## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical JIT access pattern across multiple accounts, focusing on real controls rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- Just‑in‑time access with STS and session policies
- Permissions boundaries and MFA enforcement
- Private connectivity (VPC endpoints) and immutable logs
- KMS key rotation and audit integrity
- Drift detection and revocation mechanisms

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT:role/ContractorJITRole",
      "Condition": {"BoolIfPresent": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::shared-tenant-bucket-prefix",
        "arn:aws:s3:::shared-tenant-bucket-prefix/*"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::PARTNER:role/PortalUser"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"sts:ExternalId": "tenant-xyz"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the JIT workflow under heavy churn and revocation scenarios?
- How would you validate that session policies never permit privilege creep during audits?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design automated, cross-account containment with tenant isolation, key lifecycle control, and immutable logs. Demonstrates threat modeling, policy boundaries, and incident runbook integration.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation across multiple AWS accounts
- Tenant-scoped KMS keys with rotation and key policy constraints
- Anomaly detection via CloudTrail Insights and GuardDuty
- Automated containment using EventBridge + Step Functions
- Immutable audit trails in CloudTrail and S3

## Code Example
```json
// Example KMS Key Policy (tenant-scoped)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Id": "tenant-key-policy",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_ID:role/tenant-access"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt", "kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"aws:SourceAccount": "TENANT_ACCOUNT"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end before production?
- What metrics signal health of the containment and prevent false positives?
